368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 158 0 R
/T (Several safeguards protect the stored data. The report form refuses to save a report unless the item name, place and contact are filled in. When saving, the program first writes the complete list to a temporary file and only then replaces items.dat, so an interruption during the write cannot leave a half-written file. When loading, a missing items file gives an empty list, a damaged items file is ignored instead of crashing the program, and CSV rows that do not have the expected number of fields are skipped. These behaviours were checked with a small test program that exercises the real DataStore and SearchService classes \(Chapter 6, Table 6.1\). The tests confirm that saved reports reload with their fields intact, that the recovered status persists, that images are copied with their extension kept, that a malformed CSV row is skipped and that a corrupt items file is handled without an error.)
/E (Several safeguards protect the stored data. The report form refuses to save a report unless the item name, place and contact are filled in. When saving, the program first writes the complete list to a temporary file and only then replaces items.dat, so an interruption during the write cannot leave a half-written file. When loading, a missing items file gives an empty list, a damaged items file is ignored instead of crashing the program, and CSV rows that do not have the expected number of fields are skipped. These behaviours were checked with a small test program that exercises the real DataStore and SearchService classes \(Chapter 6, Table 6.1\). The tests confirm that saved reports reload with their fields intact, that the recovered status persists, that images are copied with their extension kept, that a malformed CSV row is skipped and that a corrupt items file is handled without an error.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
