/Title (Each lost or found report is stored as a LostItem object with the following fields: a unique identifier, the type \(LOST or FOUND\), item name, category, description, place, contact details, image path \(optional\), date reported and a recovered flag. Lines in the CSV files that begin with # are treated as comments and ignored. Example rows are shown below.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2029 0 R
/Next 2036 0 R
>>
endobj
2036 0 obj
<<
/Title (The sample campus entries supplied with the application are placeholders. The campus security and medical room numbers are set to 0000000000 and must be replaced with the real numbers before use. The numbers 112, 100, 101 and 108 are the national emergency, police, fire and ambulance numbers.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2029 0 R
/Prev 2035 0 R
>>
endobj
2037 0 obj
<<
/Title (LOC8 follows a layered design that separates what the user sees from the rules of the application and from the way data are stored. The Presentation Layer consists of the Swing window and its panels. The Logic Layer consists of the model classes and the SearchService. The Data Layer consists of the DataStore class, which is the only class that reads or writes files. Because of this separation, the storage method could be replaced later \(for example by a database\) by changing DataStore alone.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2031 0 R
/Next 2038 0 R
>>
endobj
